WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.569 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:03.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, everybody,
and welcome back.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:07.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is "Corrections"
for the week of June 7th,

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:11.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you will notice we changed
the title card once again.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please let us know
what you think about it.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.549 line:92%
Although, as we've established

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:19.686 line:92%
you do not need an invitation.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:26.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want to start -- I said --
I referred to a peacock

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:27.727 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
as a she.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.230 size:81% position:41% line:92%
A peacock is the male.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.466 size:78% position:39% line:92%
A peahen is a female.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:37.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And now, colloquially,
I was correct.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
But of course,
the "Corrections" jackals

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
have a different
set of standards.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.045 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I'm glad
we cleaned that up.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.116
Last week, this happened
actually in "Corrections,"

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think one of
the most frustrating things --

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
not that this is frustrating
because, again,

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.923 size:84% position:42% line:92%
it brings me great joy.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.225 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.728 size:81% position:41%
A lot of times, I have
to correct something

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I said last week
in "Corrections."

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:08.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I talked about
how back in the old days,

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would,
with your calculator,

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:15.542 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
type 5-8-0-0-8

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:18.478 line:92%
and then flip it upside down

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.013 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and it spelled "boobs."

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:20.880 line:92%
And then I said it was great

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
because then if the teache
came over and said,

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.587 size:78% position:39% line:92%
"What are you doing?"

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would say, "Oh, we're just
doing the 80,000s."

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:33.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
People pointed out to me,
you would want to say to her

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.229 line:92%
you're doing the 58,000s.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.730 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Because you can't --

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.666 line:92%
If you give it away pre-flip

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:41.367 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
the gig is up.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.836 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was also reminded --
and I had remembered this.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
People said,
"Hey, did you forget

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:53.680 size:88% position:44% line:92%
that if you do 5,318,008

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
and flip it upside down,
it's 'boobies'"?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they said,
"Why didn't you do that?"

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:03.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like,
I'm not going to infantilize

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.758 size:88% position:44% line:92%
the human body that way.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.261 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:18.538 line:92%
I was doing my excellent,

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.173 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
unimpeachably good

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.076 line:92%
Werner Herzog impression,

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:27.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I had him say,
"Cock a doodle doo"

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:29.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
the way I thought
he would say it.

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:35.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
An eagle-eyed and helpful
German viewer

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.489 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
said, in Germany,

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.891 line:92%
it's not cock a doodle doo

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:43.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
In Germany, roosters say
"kikeriki."

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.932 align:start size:94% position:6%
Which, I just think
that's great to know.

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:50.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you're ever on vacation
in Frankfurt or Bremen,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:54.541
you know, the sun comes up
and you hear "Kikeriki!"

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:59.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now you won't lose your mind
You'll know.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:02.515 size:84% position:42%
And if it's in Bremen
and you hear a rooster,

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:06.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
it might be one of
the musicians of Bremen.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.755 line:92%
Of course it won't, though

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they never
make it to Bremen.

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.660 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Nice try.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.663 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Know ya Grimms'.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:22.268 size:88% position:44%
Some people have asked
why we bleep curse words

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:25.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
in "Corrections,"
since it's only online.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:28.841
And that's a very good question.
I don't know the answer to that.

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:33.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'll be honest, I didn't eve
know that was the case.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not going back
and watching this.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.550 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know what I mean?
Like, this is...

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:43.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
For it -- For me, the minute
this is over, it's...

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know, we'll see,
maybe I'll -- I'm gonna try,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:53.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
see if I can sneak some curses
in and we'll see...

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
and see what gets beeped,
you know?

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:58.037 line:92%
See what the standards are

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:00.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mentioned --
I got it right tonight,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.408 line:92%
but I got it wrong yesterday -

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:08.915 size:78% position:39%
West Virginia senator
Shelley Moore Capito.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:12.418 line:92%
I said "Ca-pee-to" yesterday

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.686 size:75% position:38% line:92%
I want to apologize.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then Baze --
Alex Baze, our head writer

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:22.562
Sometimes he is aware that he is
writing for someone who flubs,

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.497 line:92%
gets some pronunciations wrong

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:27.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he gave me
a very helpful way

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
to remember
the right way to do it,

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is she belongs
to the party

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
that inspired
the riot at the Capito.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.542 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've talked a lot
about the fact

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Donald Trump had a blog
and he called the horse

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:48.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
that won the Kentucky Derb
a junkie.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:49.555 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And we mentioned

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.291 size:88% position:44%
that one of the things
that made it extra funny

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:54.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
is he spelled junkie wrong -
he did J-U-N-K-Y,

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
when everybody spells it
with an I-E.

00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:01.000
Well, I got a correction
that pointed out correctly

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
that William S. Burroughs
wrote a book --

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:04.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I even went
and got the book.

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:08.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
He wrote the book "Junky"
and he spelled it with a "Y.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:11.377 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And I don't think

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
Donald Trump reads
a lot of fiction,

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:16.249 size:84% position:42% line:92%
but I bet when he does,

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:18.551 line:92%
it's William S. Burroughs.

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:22.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Trump] William S. Burr--
we love him.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.757 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Billy Burroughs.

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:25.858 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
One of the great...

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.227 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
The "S", for me --

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:30.330 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"spectacular."

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.432 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"Naked Lunch."

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.200 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
What a book.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not what I thought
it was gonna be.

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:40.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I picked it,
I thought it was gonna be

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:42.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
about one of those
Las Vegas brunch places

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:45.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
where the waitresses
don't wear tops.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:49.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it was so,
so much better than that.

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:51.884 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Billy Burroughs!

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.154 line:92%
[Norma voice] Oh, I, um...

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.055 size:78% position:39% line:92%
This one really hurts

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:58.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it's about
Marjorie Taylor Greene,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:00.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
and more often than not,
you know,

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:02.962 line:92%
she and I are on the same page

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:04.764 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:05.598 size:84% position:42% line:92%
We showed a clip of her

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:09.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
saying in defense
of Confederate statues, that -

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:13.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically, she was saying
at some town meeting

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:16.676
that if there was a
Hitler statue or a Satan statue,

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:18.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
she wouldn't want them
torn down,

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:19.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
because she would want
to use them

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:23.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
to teach her kids
about Hitler and Satan,

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:24.717 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
because, um...

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:28.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess there are
no books in her town.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:32.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said, you know,
there are no --

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:35.428 size:75% position:38%
there are no statues
of Hitler or Satan.

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:38.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then people said
there was, in Detroit --

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:41.768 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
there was a statue

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:44.937 size:81% position:41% line:92%
of a goat-headed demon

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:47.440 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
named Baphomet.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
And again, this one
you obviously want to...

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
I hope
I'm getting that one right

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:54.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I would assume that,
you know,

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:56.682 align:start size:94% position:6%
a goat-headed demon
might be a stickler

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.151 size:88% position:44% line:92%
about the pronunciation.

00:06:58.151 --> 00:07:00.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or he's laid-back,
in which case,

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:03.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
all of you
are worse than Baphomet.

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:06.359 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:12.298 line:92%
Baphomet -- goat-headed demon,

00:07:12.298 --> 00:07:14.500 line:92%
and you knew he was there,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
you knew he had arrived
via pentagram.

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:20.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know how things
like this show up.

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:22.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
You would know Baphomet
had arrived

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.644 line:92%
when you heard, "Maaahhh!"

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:28.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you would know
he'd arrived

00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:29.182 size:81% position:41% line:92%
in Germany if he said,

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:32.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Riketiki tock tock tock
tock tock-tock tock!"

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:35.521 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:45.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're talking a lot
about cicadas, and...

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:48.568 size:88% position:44%
a press plane that was
following the president,

00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:49.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
President Biden,
couldn't even take off

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:53.506
because a bunch of cicadas
caused mechanical failure.

00:07:53.506 --> 00:07:55.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been talking about
this musical "Cicada, Cicada

00:07:55.675 --> 00:07:57.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're doing,
and a lot of people said,

00:07:57.977 --> 00:08:01.214
"Oh, my God, you have to put
that in 'Cicada, Cicada.'"

00:08:01.214 --> 00:08:03.416 line:92%
"Cicada, Cicada" is done.

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:04.884 line:92%
This isn't just, like, an idea

00:08:04.884 --> 00:08:07.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've written the musical
"Cicada, Cicada."

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:11.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is about a cicada
who emerges from the soil

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
after 17 years, played by
Christine Baranski,

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:16.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
who loves musicals
and as soon as she emerges

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:19.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
immediately goes to New York
where she meets

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:22.068 line:92%
a cigarette-smoking cockroach,

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.570 line:92%
played by Harvey Fierstein

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.739 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
who loves the Mets,

00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:30.243
and he has to break it to he
that Broadway is not open yet.

00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:33.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she said, "That's fine
I'll wait as long as it takes,

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:34.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he doesn't have
the heart to tell her

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
that she's only going to liv
another two weeks.

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.718 line:92%
Like, that's how cicadas work.

00:08:38.718 --> 00:08:39.418 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And so then he --

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:43.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
a bug who has never cared
for another insect --

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:45.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
gets Nathan Lane,
played by Nathan Lane,

00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:50.229
to gather his Broadway friends
and do one show for his friend

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.764 size:84% position:42% line:92%
before she passes away.

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:55.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, after she dies,
the last scene,

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
he goes to a Mets game
by himself

00:08:57.803 --> 00:09:02.375 size:81% position:41%
and for the first time
in his life, he cries.

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:04.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not because
his friend is dead,

00:09:04.844 --> 00:09:08.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
but because the Mets blow
a four-run lead in the ninth

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:11.150 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:16.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the final song
is a torch song

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:19.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
called "Where Have You Gone,
John Franco?"

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.961 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:28.768 size:75% position:38%
And when you mention
Christine Baranski

00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:30.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
and John Franco
in the same bit,

00:09:30.036 --> 00:09:33.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is a calculus that I like
to define as

00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:36.309 line:92%
"one for me, one for the crew.

00:09:36.309 --> 00:09:38.511 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:47.787
In that song, "Where Have
You Gone, John Franco" --

00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:50.222 line:92%
'Cause critics have seen it.

00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:54.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're doing -- We've already
done an out-of-town run.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a lot of the critics
complain that

00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:57.897 size:84% position:42%
there's this whole play
about the friendship

00:09:57.897 --> 00:09:59.966
between these two bugs
and then the whole ending

00:09:59.966 --> 00:10:03.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
is about, like, complainin
about the Mets.

